## Recorded by ruling (Tony, 2026-11-11, SEQ 619)

32. 3D flats. Expand 2D drawing into 3D: the lines, rectangles and paths of drawing placed in 3D space (each piece a flat with a depth and tilt), with a movable camera producing the 2D result. No triangle meshes, no GPU pipeline: project the path points, sort pieces back to front (painter's order, matching the forward-only painter), and draw through today's floor. Circles project to ellipses; Bézier curves are split a few times under perspective. Shading from gradients and layering, as in old GUI drawing; gradients projected through their end points. First target: Tony's panel paintings as flats in space, giving parallax as the camera moves. Portraits later, with an anime-like target, not Rembrandt: layered cels give volume for small camera moves, and big turns are drawn again, as anime does. Projection math is small enough to be kant.

