## Recorded by ruling (Tony, 2026-11-14, SEQ 661; opinion only, nothing built)

34. Text poured into a form (idea 2, for tables whose cells wrap). The data: lines of text, a line end closes a row, a tab closes a piece; the Reference's rows and a DB query's results both arrive so (item 31).
    The form is two lines: a field written across=N down="*" holding one field. Piece c of line r goes to column c, row r. A short line leaves its last cells blank; a line with more pieces than columns refuses, naming the line; down="*" stops when the data or the space runs out, whichever is first. First customer: DB query results.
    CLOD'S READING (from incant/utilities; not measured):
    - "Data or space, whichever first" is a NEW rule. fillDownAcross's * counts by space alone (the room left divided by the member's height) and its field must hold no data at layout. So the form has to name its data and layout has to read the row count; show(form) lays out and draws in one call, with no seat between them to pour in.
    - across=N down="*" on one field goes through bothDirections: the number goes outside, so the result is N columns, each a stack of rows. Every such field warns today (F-157, shallow copies). A row is then not a field: its cells sit in N different columns.
    - WHEN ONE CELL WRAPS: nothing grows. A frame's height is set by layout before any text is measured; the floor breaks lines inside the frame at draw time; text too long is cut at the field's foot and warned by name. Every row keeps the one declared height and the neighbours are untouched. Rows of different heights need text measured before the frames are final, which is not built (fit, item 33, is the same wall), and need the row to be a field, so its cells can share a height.
    - MISSED: one repeated field gives N equal columns; the Reference's are 13, 39, 10, 16. A form already allowed does better with three lines: rows down="*", holding one row written across with a height, holding the N cells each with its own width. It needs no bothDirections, no F-157, the columns differ, and a row is a field. A heading row and scrolling (no windows yet) are not in the idea.
35. Kant fonts designed in the drawing language (HWF, Tony 2026-11-14). A font is a registry of glyph fields, each a drawing with an advance width; colour comes free; calligraphic styles (an English font in an Arabic or Japanese manner) need a pen with a nib angle and a varying width. Waits on stroke drawing. A PDF is written as shapes, so such glyphs would stay sharp at any size.

